---
// Part 2 — copy from "Copy update 28 Sept" (docs/), one 70ch column. Each of
// the doc's section breaks opens on a full-bleed <Scene>; the photographs are
// from Unsplash (credits in src/assets/story/README.md).
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Scene from '../components/Scene.astro';
import ReadProgress from '../components/ReadProgress.astro';
import crossing from '../assets/story/hope-01-dover-from-the-sea.jpg';
import vigil from '../assets/story/hope-02-candles.jpg';
import council from '../assets/story/hope-03-council-discussion.jpg';
import listening from '../assets/story/hope-04-listening-tea.jpg';
import assembly from '../assets/story/hope-05-hand-raised.jpg';
import young from '../assets/story/hope-06-westminster.jpg';
---

<Layout title="Our Real Hope — We The People" introLines={['Our Real', 'Hope']}>
  <PageHero lines={['Our Real', 'Hope']}>
    <p class="hero__kicker fade-up">Part 02</p>
  </PageHero>

  <ReadProgress />
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="lead">
      We believe the work starts with the issue that most urgently threatens our collective
      life.
    </p>

    <Scene
      src={crossing}
      alt="The white cliffs of Dover seen from the Channel, a ferry crossing beneath them"
      heading="Immigration"
    />
    <p>On nothing else are our divisions as deep or as dangerous.</p>
    <p>If we cannot leave it to politicians to resolve, then where do we turn?</p>

    <Scene
      src={vigil}
      alt="A crowd of people of all ages holding candles together at night"
      heading="The answer is us. We The People ourselves."
    />
    <p>
      We are the ones who must work this out, not our compromised, ego-driven political
      class.
    </p>
    <p>Only then will the decisions and policies we choose feel legitimate to us all.</p>
    <p>
      For that to happen, we must rediscover our ability to speak openly and deeply listen
      to one another's perspective, despite the depth of our differences.
    </p>
    <p>
      To see our opponents as human. To allow them to change our perspective. To seek
      compromises we can all live with.
    </p>
    <p>That is real democracy.</p>
    <p>In these polarised times, it probably seems impossible.</p>
    <p>We The People offers a practical mechanism to make it happen.</p>

    <Scene
      src={council}
      alt="A mixed group of people sitting close together in discussion, one woman speaking"
      kicker="We call it"
      heading="A Council of the People"
      focus="50% 40%"
    />
    <p>
      From two people to ten, from community level to nationwide, a Council of the People
      is a way for us to talk to each other again with honesty and respect, to find a way
      through our deep divides.
    </p>
    <p>
      Whatever the scale, the principles are the same: everyone is welcome and every point
      of view can be spoken and heard, without fear of censure.
    </p>
    <p>
      For smaller groups, we offer a clear step-by-step process, principles and resources
      to do it yourself.
    </p>
    <p>
      In larger groups, we provide impartial facilitation to support the process, creating
      room for understanding, respect — even empathy.
    </p>
    <p>
      (More details on the different models can be found in
      <a href="/be-part-of-it">Part 3 – Be Part of It</a>.)
    </p>

    <Scene
      src={listening}
      alt="An older woman smiling as she talks over a cup of tea, listened to across the table"
      heading="Truth & Reconciliation"
      focus="50% 30%"
    />
    <p>
      Whatever your perspective, in Council you will likely hear things you don't agree
      with, which might make you furious or upset. And that will be hard.
    </p>
    <p>
      But this is the heart of the process, the leap we're asking everyone to make: if you
      want your story and your perspective to be honoured, you must honour others when it's
      their turn.
    </p>
    <p>
      Through the rollout of our mechanism, we will seek to spark a movement of national
      reconnection and reconciliation, culminating in a major cultural event: a National
      Council of the People on Immigration.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first time in which we the people ourselves will write our own policy on
      immigration, instead of politicians.
    </p>

    <Scene
      src={assembly}
      alt="A man in a packed room raising his hand to speak"
      heading="Drawing on new and ancient foundations"
      focus="65% 30%"
    />
    <p>
      Councils of the People are inspired by a modern democratic model: a Citizens'
      Assembly.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is a 21st century version of an age-old democratic structure, with roots in
      ancient Greece and the moots once held on these islands a thousand years ago.
    </p>
    <p>
      In simple terms a Citizens' Assembly applies the principles of jury service to
      politics.
    </p>
    <p>
      A group of 100 or more everyday people are selected by lottery to reflect the
      population as a whole by factors like class, age, gender, race etc.
    </p>
    <p>
      They are given a difficult problem to face, like immigration. They listen to experts
      and witnesses from all sides of the debate and then talk among themselves to forge
      policy approaches that everyone can accept.
    </p>
    <p>In the last decade, Assemblies have been held all over the world.</p>
    <p>
      In Ireland, one helped people navigate the question of abortion. In France, on
      assisted dying. We even had one in the UK on climate change that led to rare
      agreement between left and right.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it works</h2>
    <p>
      Crucially, Citizens' Assemblies enshrine the idea that everyday people should make
      the decisions that affect our lives, not a political elite.
    </p>
    <p>
      That free from the pressures to win votes, funding or influence, our decisions have
      greater legitimacy too.
    </p>
    <p>
      Most importantly, that in the right circumstances, with impartial facilitation and
      expert witness testimony, we can dialogue with intelligence and civility and agree a
      more nuanced approach than any politician ever could.
    </p>

    <Scene
      src={young}
      alt="The Houses of Parliament and Westminster Bridge across the Thames under a bright sky"
      heading="We start to believe in “us” again"
    />
    <p>
      Of course, there will be hard compromises. Undoubtedly everyone will find some part
      hard to stomach.
    </p>
    <p>
      Yet we believe it will lead to the first ever policy approach that most people would
      accept.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a practical path for us to stop being a collection of tribes and start
      becoming a people again, capable of forging a shared vision for our future.
    </p>
    <p>
      If it works, we will know there is a powerful new way to do politics. One where we
      take the lead.
    </p>
    <p>This is how our system could be transformed.</p>

    <p class="pull">This is how we turn things around.</p>

    <a class="back" href="/be-part-of-it">Be Part of It →</a>
  </article>
</Layout>

<script is:inline>
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function () {
    var back = document.querySelector('article.prose .back');
    if (!back) return;
    var fired = false;
    var observer = new IntersectionObserver(function (entries) {
      entries.forEach(function (entry) {
        if (entry.isIntersecting && !fired) {
          fired = true;
          window.posthog?.capture('hope_article_read');
          observer.disconnect();
        }
      });
    }, { threshold: 0.5 });
    observer.observe(back);
  });
</script>
